---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import CaseStudyHero from "../../components/CaseStudyHero.astro";
import CaseStudyMeta from "../../components/CaseStudyMeta.astro";
import ImageBlock from "../../components/ImageBlock.astro";
import KeyDecision from "../../components/KeyDecision.astro";
import ResultCards from "../../components/ResultCards.astro";
import CaseStudyNextLink from "../../components/CaseStudyNextLink.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../../components/SiteHeader.astro";
---

<Layout
	title="From Catalog Browsing to Fit-Guided Shopping | Veronica Semeco"
	description="Case study by Veronica Semeco on the HaisaBikini Shopify rebuild."
>
	<div class="page">
		<SiteHeader current="work" />
		<main id="main">
			<CaseStudyHero
				kicker="From catalog browsing"
				title="to fit-guided shopping"
				imagePath="/images/haisabikini/haisa-hero.png"
			/>

			<section class="intro">
				<div class="intro-top">
					<div class="body-copy intro-summary">
						<p>The goal was to translate what already worked in stores: fit advice, styling support, and confidence around coverage and support, into a clearer online shopping experience.</p>
						<p>HAISABIKINI is the online store of an authorized U.S. franchise retailer of Agua Bendita, the Colombian swimwear brand. I first defined the fit-guided strategy through an e-commerce audit, then translated it into a WooCommerce-to-Shopify rebuild.</p>
					</div>
					<CaseStudyMeta
						items={[
							{ label: "Role", value: "UX Strategy / Product Design" },
							{ label: "Timeline", value: "2 months, March–May 2026" },
							{ label: "Platform", value: "Shopify, Prestige theme" },
							{ label: "Scope", value: "E-commerce strategy, WooCommerce-to-Shopify rebuild, navigation, collections, product data cleanup, PDP improvements, SEO/analytics setup, QA" },
						]}
					/>
				</div>

				<section class="intro-card">
					<h2 class="display">Outcome</h2>
					<ResultCards
						results={[
	{
		label: "Catalog to guided shopping",
		detail: "The experience moved from generic product browsing to a fit-guided structure built around support, coverage, matching pieces, and expert help.",
	},
	{
		label: "Strategy translated into Shopify",
		detail: "The audit identified the need to make in-store fit expertise visible online. The rebuild turned that strategy into navigation, collections, PDP logic, product data, and trust cues.",
	},
	{
		label: "Launch-ready foundation",
		detail: "The project created a cleaner Shopify foundation for fit-based browsing, product merchandising, PDP guidance, SEO, analytics setup, QA, and future optimization.",
	},
]}
					/>
				</section>

				<section class="intro-card">
					<h2 class="display">Context</h2>
					<div class="body-flow">
						<p>HAISABIKINI sells Agua Bendita swimwear through physical stores and online.</p>
						<p>In store, customers get help choosing styles based on fit, coverage, support, size, body confidence, and styling. Online, that value was harder to see. The previous store behaved more like a catalog: product grids, seasonal collections, and product pages that expected shoppers to decide on their own.</p>
						<p>Customers could already find Agua Bendita elsewhere. HAISABIKINI needed to make its advantage visible online: authorized Agua Bendita access, 12 years of swimwear expertise, physical store credibility, and help choosing the right piece.</p>
				 		<p>The strategy phase identified the core opportunity: move from product-first browsing to guidance-first shopping.</p>
					</div>
					<ImageBlock src="/images/haisabikini/haisa-homepage-before-after.png" alt="HAISABIKINI homepage before and after" aspect="shallow" />
				</section>

				<section class="intro-card">
					<h2 class="display">The challenge</h2>
					<div class="body-flow">
						<p>How do you make an online swimwear store feel less like a generic catalog and more like a guided fit experience without delaying launch or building a custom fit tool?</p>
						<p>Shoppers needed help answering practical questions: Which top gives more support? Which bottom gives more coverage? How do I find the matching piece? Do these patterns coordinate? Is there a real person who can help me choose?</p>
						<p>The work had real constraints: messy product data, thousands of product images, Shopify theme limits, and an Odoo integration outside my scope before launch.</p>
					</div>
				</section>

				<section class="intro-card">
					<h2 class="display">My role</h2>
					<div class="body-flow">
						<p>I led the strategy and UX structure from the initial e-commerce audit through the Shopify rebuild.</p>
						<p>I focused on:</p>
						<ul>
							<li>Defining the shift from product-first to guidance-first shopping.</li>
							<li>Restructuring navigation and collections.</li>
							<li>Creating product data logic for product type, gender, support, coverage, print, and matching relationships.</li>
							<li>Defining how support and coverage appear across PDPs and collections.</li>
							<li>Designing PDP guidance logic for size guidance, WhatsApp support, matching pieces, same-collection recommendations, and trust signals.</li>
							<li>Scoping phase one around a strong Shopify foundation instead of an overbuilt concept.</li>
						</ul>
						<p>I did not own Odoo/ERP debugging, full custom development, Meta shop migration, or post-launch analytics. Since the site has not launched yet, there are no conversion or revenue results to claim.</p>
					</div>
				</section>
			</section>

			<section class="decisions">
				<KeyDecision
					number={1}
					title="Make fit guidance the strategic direction"
					paragraphs={[
						"The audit showed that the old site listed products and collections, but did not clearly explain why a shopper should buy here instead of through Agua Bendita’s main site or another retailer.",
						"I recommended positioning HAISABIKINI around swimwear guidance. The store still needed to sell Agua Bendita clearly, but the stronger digital role became helping customers choose the right fit with more confidence.",
					]}
		
					imagePath="/images/haisabikini/haisa-fit-collections.png"
				/>

				<KeyDecision
					number={2}
					title="Structure navigation around shopping intent"
					paragraphs={[
						"The previous browsing structure leaned on catalog and collection logic. I restructured navigation around the way shoppers choose swimwear: product type, support, coverage, and matching pieces.",
						"“Find Your Fit” gave shoppers a guidance-oriented path without requiring a custom quiz, while Men and Kids stayed simpler because the fit logic was less complex there.",
					]}
					changes={[
						"Women’s swimwear was separated into Bikini Tops, Bikini Bottoms, One Pieces, Find Your Fit, and Ready to Wear.",
						"Fit became a browsing concept through support and coverage paths.",
						"Support and coverage filters applied where they made sense for bikinis and swimsuits.",
						"Men and Kids stayed simpler because the fit logic was less complex there.",
						"Matching pieces became easier to reach from the product journey.",
					]}
					imagePath="/images/haisabikini/haisa-navigation-structure.png"
				/>

				<KeyDecision
					number={3}
					title="Make product data do UX work"
					paragraphs={[
						"The fit-guided experience depended on cleaner product data. I structured the product logic around category, gender, product type, support, coverage, print, and matching relationships.",
						"Tags supported collection and filtering logic. Metafields supported cleaner PDP display logic, especially for support and coverage. This became the bridge between the fit-guided strategy and the Shopify implementation.",
					]}
					changes={[
						"Product type determined which fit information appeared on the PDP.",
						"Support became filterable for bikini tops.",
						"Coverage became filterable for bikini bottoms and one-pieces.",
						"Tags supported collection and filtering logic.",
						"Metafields supported cleaner PDP display logic.",
						"Print and collection logic supported matching products and same-collection recommendations.",
					]}
					imagePath="/images/haisabikini/haisa-product-data-ux-logic.png"
				/>

				<KeyDecision
					number={4}
					title="Turn the PDP into a decision page"
					paragraphs={[
						"The old PDP showed the product, but did not support the full swimwear decision: fit, size, matching pieces, and help if the shopper was unsure.",
						"The new PDP introduced product-type-specific fit logic, clearer sizing, directional matching labels, WhatsApp stylist help, same-collection recommendations, and trust signals.",
						"For tops, the matching section says “Bottoms designed to match.” For bottoms, it says “Tops designed to match.” This made the relationship clearer than a generic related-products module.",
					]}
					changes={[
						"Bikini top PDPs can show support.",
						"Bikini bottom PDPs can show coverage.",
						"One-piece PDPs can show both support and coverage.",
						"Matching pieces are clearer and more directional.",
						"Same-collection recommendations clarify related products.",
						"WhatsApp appears as support for the shopping decision.",
						"Trust signals reinforce store credibility and purchase confidence.",
					]}
					imagePath="/images/haisabikini/haisa-pdp-fit-module.png"
				/>

				<KeyDecision
					number={5}
					title="Keep phase one focused"
					paragraphs={[
						"Lightweight feedback from three people sharpened the final execution, especially around naming, product type clarity, matching logic, and whether pieces from the same collection coordinate.",
						"There were also bigger ideas: a fit quiz, richer product education, image reordering, advanced recommendations, custom analytics, and a fuller mix-and-match guide. For phase one, I protected the Shopify foundation instead of trying to ship every possible idea.",
						"The final scope focused on Shopify setup, navigation, collections, product data cleanup, PDP guidance logic, fit-based browsing, basic SEO, GA4, domain connection, light QA, and launch support.",
					]}
					imagePath="/images/haisabikini/haisa-phase-one-next-phase.png"
				/>
			</section>

			<section class="outro">
				<section>
					<h2 class="display">What I would improve next</h2>
					<div class="body-flow">
						<p>Once the site is live and the Odoo integration is stable, I would focus on:</p>
						<ul>
							<li>Measuring engagement with fit collections and support/coverage filters.</li>
							<li>Understanding where shoppers drop off when completing a bikini set.</li>
							<li>Improving product naming so silhouette and product type are easier to scan.</li>
							<li>Reordering images so the item being sold is clear earlier.</li>
							<li>Tracking WhatsApp clicks from PDPs.</li>
							<li>Clarifying collection-based matching and mix-and-match logic.</li>
							<li>Improving filters for ready-to-wear and accessories.</li>
							<li>Testing whether a more guided fit flow is worth building later.</li>
						</ul>
						<p>The foundation was built so future improvements can be based on evidence, not guesses.</p>
					</div>
				</section>
				<CaseStudyNextLink
					label="Continue to:"
					href="/work/navit-platform/"
					text="Redesigning NAVIT’s mobility platform →"
				/>
			</section>
		</main>
		<SiteFooter />
	</div>
</Layout>

<style>
	.intro {
		display: grid;
		gap: var(--space-8);
		padding: var(--space-11) var(--layout-page-padding) var(--space-12);
	}

	.intro-top {
		display: grid;
		gap: var(--space-8);
		grid-template-columns: minmax(0, var(--layout-measure)) var(--layout-aside);
		justify-content: space-between;
	}

	.intro-summary,
	.intro-card,
	.additional,
	.outro section {
		display: grid;
		gap: var(--space-8);
	}

	.intro-summary {
		align-content: start;
		gap: var(--space-3);
	}

	.intro-card {
		max-width: var(--layout-measure);
		padding-top: var(--space-11);
	}

	.intro-card h2,
	.outro h2 {
		font-size: var(--text-l);
	}

	.intro-card p,
	.intro-card li,
	.additional p,
	.outro p,
	.outro li {
		color: var(--color-text-primary);
		font-size: var(--text-s);
		line-height: var(--leading-ui);
	}

	.body-flow {
		display: grid;
		gap: var(--space-3);
	}

	.body-flow ul {
		margin: 0;
		padding-left: var(--space-6);
	}

	.body-flow li + li {
		margin-top: var(--space-2);
	}

	.decisions {
		padding: 0 var(--layout-page-padding);
	}

	.decisions :global(.key-decision) {
		border-top: 0.5px solid var(--color-line-secondary);
	}

	.additional {
		padding: var(--space-11) var(--layout-page-padding);
	}

	.additional :global(.callout),
	.additional :global(.image-block) {
		max-width: var(--layout-measure);
	}

	.outro {
		padding: 0 var(--layout-page-padding);
	}

	.outro section {
		padding: var(--space-11) 0;
	}

	.outro section:last-child {
		padding-bottom: var(--space-12);
	}

	.outro p,
	.outro ul,
	.outro :global(.result-cards) {
		max-width: var(--layout-measure);
	}

	.outro ul {
		margin: 0;
		padding-left: var(--space-6);
	}

	@media (max-width: 760px) {
		.intro-top {
			grid-template-columns: 1fr;
		}

		.intro {
			padding: var(--space-11) var(--layout-page-padding) var(--space-12);
		}

		.intro-card {
			gap: var(--space-6);
			padding-top: var(--space-9);
		}

		.additional,
		.outro section {
			gap: var(--space-6);
		}

		.intro-card h2,
		.outro h2 {
			font-size: var(--text-mm);
			font-weight: 500;
		}
	}
</style>